    "content": "# Text access to the original historical document\n\nStatus: historical proposal; equations and layout should be checked in the original download. Text extraction is an access aid, not a new derivation.\n\n Distinction Dynamics: Emergent Geometry, Matter,\n   and Gauge Interactions from a Packet Routing\n                     Substrate\n                                           Benjamin Mayes1\n                                     1\n                                         Independent Researcher\n\n                                                 2026\n\n\n                                                Abstract\n        Distinction Dynamics is a substrate-based framework in which spacetime, matter, and\n     gauge interactions emerge from a discrete network of interacting packets carrying internal\n     routing states. Transport between neighboring packets is mediated by link operators whose\n     holonomy defines curvature. Localized routing defects correspond to matter excitations,\n     while internal routing symmetries generate gauge interactions. Coarse-graining of packet\n     connectivity yields an emergent metric whose curvature obeys Einstein-like gravitational\n     dynamics. Odd-framed defects obey Dirac-like transport equations, whereas framing-trivial\n     packet excitations obey Klein–Gordon-type dynamics. Scalar packet-support backgrounds\n     generate symmetry breaking and effective particle masses through Yukawa-like couplings.\n     Quantization of packet transport operators yields discrete curvature spectra, providing a\n     candidate description of quantum geometry. The paper presents the canonical structure of\n     the theory, its continuum field limits, a computational packet-lattice model, and a first set of\n     qualitative observational consequences.\n\n\n1. Introduction\n\nA central unresolved problem in theoretical physics is the simultaneous explanation of quantum\nmatter, gauge interactions, and gravity within a single ontology. Standard quantum field theory\nassumes a differentiable background geometry, while general relativity treats that geometry\nas dynamical but classical. Distinction Dynamics proposes a different starting point: physical\nreality is fundamentally a discrete packet-routing substrate. Geometry, matter, and gauge sectors\nare not inserted independently; rather, they arise from the dynamics of packet states and link\ntransport.\n    The theory is motivated by five structural goals:\n  (i) derive curvature from transport mismatch around packet loops,\n\n (ii) identify matter as localized routing defects,\n\n(iii) derive gauge sectors from internal routing symmetries,\n\n (iv) recover continuum gravity and field theory by coarse-graining, and\n\n (v) formulate a computational model capable of testing emergent behavior directly.\n    The present manuscript consolidates the full current structure of Distinction Dynamics into\na journal-style form with explicit equations and illustrative figures.\n\n                                                    1\n\f2. Foundational Postulates\n\nPostulate 1 (Packet substrate). Reality is composed of discrete interacting packets arranged on\na dynamical network. Each packet i carries state variables\n\n                                           Xi = (ρi , θi , χi ),                             (1)\n\nwhere ρi is burden density, θi is an internal orientation/framing variable, and χi is a closure-\nbranch variable.\n\nPostulate 2 (Seam transport). Neighboring packets are connected by transport operators Uij\ncarrying internal routing information between packets i and j.\n\nPostulate 3 (Curvature as transport mismatch). Curvature is defined by holonomy mismatch\nof transport around closed packet loops. For a loop C, the transport holonomy is\n                                                     Y\n                                           UC =             Uij .                            (2)\n                                 