= 2g µν I.                                  (16)\n\nSquaring Eq. (15) yields the spin-curvature coupling,\n\n                                               q\n                                 \u0012                         \u0013\n                                     D2 + m2ψ + Σµν Fµν ψ = 0,                                  (17)\n                                               2\n\nwith Σµν = 2i [Γµ , Γν ].\n\n\n\n\n                                                  4\n\f6.3. Framing-trivial bosonic excitations\nFraming-trivial packet excitations are described by a scalar field ϕ obeying\n\n                                        (Dµ Dµ + m2ϕ )ϕ = 0.                                  (18)\n\nIf the scalar is neutral, q = 0, Eq. (18) reduces to the neutral Klein–Gordon equation. Charged\nframing-trivial bosons provide the natural microscopic bridge to condensate and superconducting\norder-parameter sectors.\n\n7. Gauge Sectors from Internal Routing Symmetries\n\nInternal routing generators satisfy\n\n                                         [T a , T b ] = if abc T c .                          (19)\n\nThe multi-component connection is\n                                             Aµ = Aaµ T a ,                                   (20)\nand the non-abelian field strength is\n\n                                 a\n                                Fµν = ∂µ Aaν − ∂ν Aaµ − gf abc Abµ Acν .                      (21)\n\nThe theory interprets the U(1)-like sector as single-channel seam transport, the SU(2)-like sector\nas a two-component framing basis, and the SU(3)-like sector as a three-channel cluster-routing\nbasis.\n\n8. Unified Matter–Gauge–Scalar Action\n\nThe minimal unified action is\n                                                                  1 a aµν\n             Z      \u0014                                                                  \u0015\n        S=       d4 x Ψ̄(iΓµ Dµ − m0 )Ψ + (Dµ Φ)† (Dµ Φ) − V (Φ) − Fµν F  − y Ψ̄ΦΨ .          (22)\n                  