cs may be expressed as a path integral over packet-network histories,\n                                                 X\n                                    Z=                       eiSpacket /ℏ .                     (29)\n                                           packet networks\n\nBecause holonomies of compact transport groups have discrete spectra, the theory predicts\ndiscrete curvature quanta and a minimal geometric scale.\n\n11. Cosmology\n\nOn scales much larger than the packet length ℓp , the effective geometry is approximated by a\nhomogeneous and isotropic metric,\n\n                                         ds2 = −dt2 + a(t)2 d⃗x2 .                              (30)\n\nThe emergent gravitational field equation then yields the Friedmann-like relation\n                                   \u0012 \u00132\n                                    ȧ          8πG\n                                            =       (ρm + ρr + ρΛ ).                            (31)\n                                     a           3\n\nHere ρm is defect matter density, ρr is transport radiation density, and ρΛ is residual packet-\nsupport vacuum energy. Scalar-vacuum selection triggers symmetry breaking and mass generation\nin the early universe. Topological defects may form when different regions settle into different\nvacuum orientations.\n\n\n\n\n                                                     6\n\f12. Fundamental Parameter Relations\n\nThe theory reduces fundamental constants to substrate parameters (ℓp , τp , v):\n\n                                                ℓp\n                                              c=    ,                                          (32)\n                                                τp\n                                                 ℏ\n                                           MP ∼       ,                                        (33)\n                                                cℓp\n                                                ℓ2p τp\n                                            G∼          ,                                      (34)\n                                                  ℏ\n                                           mi = yi v.                                          (35)\n\nThe speed of light is interpreted as the maximal seam transport rate, the Planck scale as the\nenergy required to localize transport to a single packet, and particle masses as routing-complexity-\ndependent couplings to the scalar packet background.\n\n13. Computational Model\n\nThe packet substrate can be simulated as a dynamic graph with node states Xi = (ρi , θi , χi ) and\nlink operators Uij . A discrete Monte Carlo evolution updates nodes and edges according to the\naction in Eq. (7). Curvature is measured by loop holonomy and emergent metric structure is\nextracted from weighted shortest-path distances.\n\n        Defect and holonomy loop                                 Simulation pipeline\n\n                                                                  Initialize lattice\n                      UC\n                                                                 Update node/edge\n                           defect\n                                                                  Measure UC , Ri\n\n\n                                                                    Track defects\n\nFigure 3: Left: localized defect and surrounding holonomy loop in the packet lattice. Right:\nschematic Monte Carlo simulation workflow used to evolve packet networks and measure emergent\nobservables.\n\n   The minimal Monte Carlo loop is:\n\nfor step in simulation_steps:\n    choose random node or edge\n    propose local update to X_i or U_ij\n    compute Delta S\n    accept with probability min(1, exp(-Delta S / T))\n    measure curvature, holonomy, and defect observables\n\n\n\n\n                                                 7\n\f14. Numerical Observables\n\nThe principal numerical observables are:\n                      X\n               Ri =         Tr(I − UC ),                                                        (36)\n                      C∋i\n             C(r) = ⟨Xi Xi+r ⟩,                                                                 (37)\n                       Y\n              ΦC =             Uij ,                                                            (38)\n                      (ij)∈C\n\n            E(k) : defect dispersion extracted from Fourier analysis of trajectories.           (39)\n\nExpected signatures include:\n\n (a) stable localized defect trajectories,\n\n (b) nontrivial holonomy phases for transport around twisted loops,\n\n (c) curvature localized around defects,\n\n (d) attractive defect–defect interactions with gravitational-like scaling,\n\n (e) topological vortex or string configurations identified by winding number.\n\n15. Observational and Experimental Consequences\n\nThe framework predicts several potentially testable consequences:\n\n  (i) relic routing defects analogous to cosmic strings,\n\n (ii) a stochastic gravitational-wave background sourced by packet-curvature fluctuations,\n\n (iii) small deviations of dark-energy behavior from an exactly constant vacuum equation of\n       state,\n\n (iv) emergent gauge and confinement analogs in strongly correlated condensed-matter systems,\n\n (v) possible ultraviolet saturation effects associated with a minimal packet length.\n\n16. Discussion\n\nDistinction Dynamics overlaps conceptually with lattice gauge theory, loop-based quantum\ngeometry approaches, emergent gravity models, and condensed-matter analog gravity. Its\ndistinctive feature is the unified identification of packet routing as the common microscopic\norigin of matter, gauge interactions, and geometry. The theory is therefore not merely a lattice\nregularization of pre-existing fields; rather, the lattice-like packet substrate is the ontology from\nwhich continuum fields emerge.\n    Several open quantitative problems remain: exp