e invariant under GλG_\\lambda. If A,B>0A,B>0 and A≠BA\\ne B, there is a nonidentity GλG_\\lambda that also preserves SS and changes [c]+[c]_+. Proof. Each adjacent product gains the factor λλ−1=1\\lambda\\lambda^{-1}=1. The transformed norm is Aλ2+Bλ−2A\\lambda^2+B\\lambda^{-2}. Setting y=λ2y=\\lambda^2, equality with A+BA+B gives Ay2−(A+B)y+B=(y−1)(Ay−B)=0. Ay^2-(A+B)y+B=(y-1)(Ay-B)=0. Besides y=1y=1, there is y=B/A≠1y=B/A\\ne1. Since both parity sectors are nonzero, positive-ray equality would require λ=λ−1\\lambda=\\lambda^{-1}, hence λ=1\\lambda=1, a contradiction. ▫\\square A rational witness is supported on vertex 00 and edge 0101. Set (c0,c01)=(2,1)(c_0,c_{01})=(2,1), all other coefficients zero. The vector (1,2)(1,2) has the same total norm 5 and the same products, but a different positive ray. With the canonical boundary sign, the nonzero current is −4-4 in both cases. Even where A=BA=B, stocks and currents cannot distinguish simultaneous sign reversal on a connected support component. The theorem establishes noninjectivity of the observation map; it does not say every state has the same ambiguity. Additional metadata cannot repair it unless that metadata is specified to constrain the ambiguous state coordinates. Labels describing an event or route, by themselves, are not such a constraint. 2.2 A second ambiguity: component signs Form a graph whose vertices are simplex coordinates with qσ>0q_\\sigma>0, and whose edges are their adjacent-degree incidences. Multiplying all coefficients in one connected component by minus one leaves all stocks and currents unchanged. Edges to zero coordinates still have zero current. There is therefore one undetermined common sign per connected support component. Fixing one global sign suffices only when that graph is connected. A zero coordinate can split the graph and increase the number of required reference signs. An isolated nonzero coordinate is a component and needs its own reference. 3. A sufficient recorded statistic