xactly in Q(√5), represented as rational pairs [a,b] for a+b√5. The interior dihedral angle at each radial edge is 2π/5, or 72 degrees. The verifier checks the face-normal dot products exactly, including their positive sign, using cos(2π/5)=5−14,cos2(2π/5)=3−58.\\cos(2\\pi/5)=\\frac{\\sqrt5-1}{4},\\qquad \\cos^2(2\\pi/5)=\\frac{3-\\sqrt5}{8}. Thus five sectors close: 5(2π/5) = 2π. Five regular Euclidean tetrahedra instead leave the strictly positive angular gap δ=2π−5arccos(1/3)>0,\\delta=2\\pi-5\\arccos(1/3)>0, because cos(2π/5) < 1/3. This is a precise obstruction to replacing the constituents of this particular central construction by congruent regular tetrahedra with the same straight-edge Euclidean realization. It is not a no-go for abstract tetrahedral incidence, other subdivisions, other metrics or curved realizations. The lengths are dimensionless coordinates of a declared embedding. They do not assign physical distance or select the chain-space inner product H. A unit simplex metric and a metric weighted by this embedding would be distinct operator choices. Integral chain verification The package specifies all labeled simplex bases and integer boundary matrices. It checks ∂² = 0 and boundary ranks 12, 30 and 20. An oriented filling t has boundary b on exactly the twenty exterior triangles; radial triangles cancel and ∂b = 0. An explicit integer cone homotopy verifies ∂h+h∂=I−ιϵ,\\partial h+h\\partial=I-\\iota\\epsilon, where ε is augmentation on vertices and ι selects the center. In positive degrees the right side is I. This proves the filled carrier has zero reduced integral homology, including the absence of torsion; rational rank calculations alone would not prove the torsion statement. The exterior is the boundary of the verified convex polyhedron, hence a two-sphere. The construction is an admissible tetrahedral representation of a larger rounded polyhedron. It does not imply that the tetrahedra are material subparticles or that the center is an experi