x=BV(τ0),∑k≥0Ck=BC(τ0).\\mathbb E N_{\\mathrm{vertex}}=B_V(\\tau_0),\\qquad \\sum_{k\\ge0}C_k=B_C(\\tau_0). For the uniform initial reference these reduce to the boxed result at the start. Expected detector uses through the first low-boundary event equal 1+2L/3. For L=0 there are no vertices or phase contrast and the first completed detector use is low boundary. L Initial contrast Expected total vertices Expected uses through first low Integrated contrast 1 1/2 2/3 5/3 1/2 2 2/3 4/3 7/3 10/9 4 4/5 8/3 11/3 12/5 8 8/9 16/3 19/3 136/27 These are expectations, not deterministic cutoffs. Repeated stay events can produce an arbitrarily long, increasingly unlikely vertex history. Vertex events can remain possible after phase contrast is zero, especially at L=1. 6. Histories, survivor conditioning and an operational stopping budget Before the first low outcome, every particular vertex label has the same reduced reference update for this harmonic workload. The unnormalized all-vertex survivor state after k completed detector uses is T^k(tau_0), with survival S_k=Tr[T^k(tau_0)]. Its normalized reference state is tau_(k|survive)=T^k(tau_0)/S_k. Failed histories are at |0><0|, so τk=𝒯k(τ0)+(1−Sk)|0⟩⟨0|,Ck=SkC(τk|survive).\\tau_k=\\mathcal T^k(\\tau_0)+(1-S_k)|0\\rangle\\langle0|,\\qquad C_k=S_k C(\\tau_{k|\\mathrm{survive}}). For k>=1 and B~Binomial(k-1,3/4), exact closed forms are Sk=1L+1∑m=1LPr(B≤m−1),Ck=1L+1∑n=2LPr(B≤n−2).S_k=\\frac1{L+1}\\sum_{m=1}^{L}\\Pr(B\\le m-1),\\qquad C_k=\\frac1{L+1}\\sum_{n=2}^{L}\\Pr(B\\le n-2). Also S_0=1 and C_0=L/(L+1). The binomial CDF is the rational sum sum_(b=0..min(k-1,r)) binom(k-1,b)3b/4(k-1); it is zero for r<0. These are ensemble predictions, not a claim that separate output systems are independent. At L=4, initial contrast4/5 becomes3/4 after one observed vertex and9/13 after two observed vertices. The corresponding unconditional contrasts are3/5 and9/20. Comparing conditional and unconditional columns without their survival weights 