    "content": "78 exact constructor checks in package; no empirical data or independent review claimed. Closed finite example · no jump law. Physical promotion 0. Current session checkpoint.Markdown source · LaTeX source · Complete packageAbstract We compare the unit-weight finite-chain operators of the two triangulations in one labeled three-dimensional bistellar move. Their chain spaces have dimensions 23 and 27. Their labeled union has dimension 30 and is the boundary of a 4-simplex, giving integral coordinate inclusions into a common space. The difference of the zero-extended skew operators has rank 18, certified by a determinant-one minor and a twelve-dimensional kernel. Both local triangulations have trivial reduced integer homology and the same oriented exterior boundary, but their operator spectra differ. We specify the changes in incidence receipts and show that the overlap projection is neither a chain map nor a norm-preserving jump rule. The calculation is a reproducible finite example, with no independent novelty claim or physical interpretation. 1. Complexes and the ambient correction All simplex labels are strictly increasing; an expression such as 01230123 denotes the oriented simplex [0,1,2,3][0,1,2,3]. Write K−=⟨0123,0124⟩,K+=⟨0134,0234,1234⟩, K_-=\\langle0123,0124\\rangle,\\qquad K_+=\\langle0134,0234,1234\\rangle, where angle brackets mean downward closure. With T=Δ(012)T=\\Delta(012) and E=Δ(34)E=\\Delta(34), these are T*∂ET*\\partial E and ∂T*E\\partial T*E, respectively. This is the standard bistellar replacement of a simplex joined to a boundary by the boundary joined to the complementary simplex [1]. The actual labeled union and intersection are U=K−∪K+=∂Δ(01234),C=K−∩K+=∂T*∂E. U=K_-\\cup K_+=\\partial\\Delta(01234),\\qquad C=K_-\\cap K_+=\\partial T*\\partial E. Thus K±K_\\pm are 3-balls, CC is their common exterior 2-sphere, and UU is a closed 3-sphere. Here UU is an abstract comparison complex; it does not assert a simultaneous nonoverlapping embedding of both triangulations into the same geometric 3-ball. Complex f0f_0 f1f_1 f2f_2 f3f_3 Total chain dimension K−K_- 5 9 7 2 23 K+K_+ 5 10 9 3 27 CC 5 9 6 0 20 UU 5 10 10 5 30 The 27-coordinate proposal cannot provide inclusions of both labeled complexes: K−K_- contains simplices absent from K+K_+. The 30-coordinate union is the smallest ambient coordinate space containing every distinct labeled simplex from both. This minimality concerns labeled coordinate inclusions, not all possible abstract embeddings. Degree Deleted generators Created generators Net change 0 none none 0 1 none 3434 1 2 012012 034,134,234034,134,234 2 3 0123,01240123,0124 0134,0234,12340134,0234,1234 1 The net budget (0,1,2,1)(0,1,2,1) is not the list of created simplices. There are three deleted coordinates and seven created coordinates. The union and ordering were fixed from these labels before any spectrum was computed. 2. Integral matrices and comparison maps For any complex XX, take C(X;ℤ)=⨁k=03Ck(X;ℤ) C(X;\\mathbb Z)=\\bigoplus_{k=0}^3 C_k(X;\\mathbb Z) with nonaugmented boundary DXD_X. For a simplex of positive degree, DX[v0,…,vk]=∑j=0k(−1)j[v0,…,v̂j,…,vk], D_X[v_0,\\ldots,v_k]=\\sum_{j=0}^k(-1)^j[v_0,\\ldots,\\widehat v_j,\\ldots,v_k], and the boundary of a vertex is zero. Use the same basis after extending scalars to ℝ\\mathbb R, with the standard unit inner product. Define AX=DXT−DX,LX=−AX2=DXDXT+DXTDX. A_X=D_X^T-D_X,\\qquad L_X=-A_X^2=D_XD_X^T+D_X^TD_X. All these matrices have integer entries. The ambient basis, ordered first by degree and then lexicographically, is ℬU=(0,1,2,3,4;01,02,03,04,12,13,14,23,24,34;012,013,014,023,024,034,123,124,134,234;0123,0124,0134,0234,1234). \\begin{split} \\mathcal B_U=(&0,1,2,3,4;\\\\ &01,02,03,04,12,13,14,23,24,34;\\\\ &012,013,014,023,024,034,123,124,134,234;\\\\ &0123,0124,0134,0234,1234). \\end{split} Native bases are the induced subsequences. Let eje_j be ambient coordinate columns with one-based indices. The inclusion matrices are