    "content": "Expository model with a tetrahedral reference implementation. Established SER/orientation-lattice mathematics; no physical metric or duration derived. Research status: physical promotion 0. No resonance measurements supplied. Current session status and scope.Download source · PDF · TeX · Complete reference packageAbstract We study integer event counts in sink reversal on a finite bipartite incidence graph, initialized with every vertex actor a sink. A height representation gives compatible firing counts, relative-clock reconstruction and an attained bound on successful events before a specified actor fires. The bound is one plus the sum of graph distances from that actor. These source results are presented as elementary specializations of established edge-reversal, marked-graph and orientation-height theory; no independent novelty is claimed. For tetrahedral incidence, a reference implementation adds finite request ports, a bounded recorder and a freeze-and-collect readout. Separate propositions state the abstraction relation and exact operation costs. The model offers a precise convention for event time in the Unified Dynamics programme. It does not derive space, a spacetime metric, physical duration or a law of motion from tetrahedral geometry. Keywords: sink reversal; bipartite incidence graphs; event counts; height functions; finite observation. 1. Question and mathematical scope The readiness dynamics is sink reversal (scheduling by edge reversal, SER) on the vertex-cell incidence graph, initially oriented toward every vertex actor. An enabled actor is a sink and firing reverses all its incident edges. The full source bits additionally retain one complement phase per connected component; Appendix D describes this distinction. The application question is how a chosen event rule defines local integer clocks and what a finite observer must retain to read them. The source mathematics belongs to established theory: marked graphs [1], edge-reversal resource sharing [3,4], and circulation-preserving orientation moves and height lattices [5,6]. We give elementary proofs to fix conventions and expose the observation assumptions. The distance-sum formula in Section 3 is a specialization of the height-range argument in [6], not a claimed new theorem. Full-reversal routing also has exact reversal-count analyses [7]; its destination-based termination model should not be identified with this recurrent source without a separate reduction. Logical clocks [2] provide context for the distinction between causal order and a scalar count. The protocol appendices describe a reference implementation assembled from familiar distributed-computing ideas. Related work includes local resource-allocation protocols [8], concurrent record reads [9], and distributed snapshots [10]. The present readout pauses source actors; it is not a Chandy-Lamport snapshot of channel state. We claim neither a new general synchronization method nor a machine-checked parameterized protocol proof. This is a fixed-carrier model. There is no stochastic law, Hamiltonian, continuous parameter, manifold reconstruction or physical clock calibration. The carrier need not be a triangulated manifold. Source events and communication operations are mathematical transitions; no bound in this paper is a wall-time deadline. A successful transition is an enabled operation that changes the modeled state; failed polls and idle attempts are excluded. A maximal execution is infinite or ends with no enabled transition. Layer Declared input Consequence studied Spatial carrier Finite bipartite incidence; tetrahedra in the implementation Incidence and overlap distances Source Binary vertex/cell registers, guards below, zero initialization Reachability and local ticks Execution Successful single-event transitions; connected carrier for global progress Operation-count bounds Local implementation One register owner; reliable bounded ports; local atomic steps Source refinement Observation Chosen anchor, routing tree, finite word and result storage Coherent bounded readout The source results use only the first three rows. Removing the observer does not remove the definition of a source tick. Adding a capacity-limited observer can restrict which source histories continue. 2. Spatial incidence and the native tick Let 𝒦\\mathcal K be a finite nonempty indexed family of finite nonempty subsets of a vertex set V, and take V to be their union. Write n=|V|n=|V|, m=|𝒦|m=|\\mathcal K|, I(i)={c:i∈c}I(i)=\\{c:i\\in c\\}, and J=∑c|c|J=\\sum_c|c|. The incidence graph G has actor set V⊔𝒦V\\sqcup\\mathcal K and edges {i,c}\\{i,c\\} whenever i∈ci\\in c. This permits any finite bipartite graph without isolated actors, including distinct cell actors with identical neighborhoods. The cell-overlap graph H joins cells sharing a vertex. Its distance is combinatorial, not a spacetime metric. C1-C3 require no embedding or fixed cell arity. Sections 5-6 and Appendices A-C use four-element cells where their numerical coefficients require J=4mJ=4m. Such cells may be completed to an abstract simplicial complex, but its lower-dimensional faces do not enter the source dynamics. Each vertex has a bit bib_i; each cell has a bit pcp_c. Initially all are zero. The only source transitions are Pi:bi=pdfor every d∈I(i),bi←1−bi,Cc:bi≠pcfor every i∈c,pc←1−pc. \\begin{aligned} P_i &: \\quad b_i=p_d\\ \\text{for every }d\\in I(i), && b_i\\leftarrow1-b_i,\\\\ C_c &: \\quad b_i\\ne p_c\\ \\text{for every }i\\in c, && p_c\\leftarrow1-p_c. \\end{aligned} Each transition changes only the indicated bit. Guards refer to the pre-state. Vertex preparation is not a cell tick. For a finite history h, let Mi(h)M_i(h) count its occurrences of PiP_i, and Nc(h)N_c(h) count its occurrences of CcC_c. Define the local native clock by τc(h)=Nc(h)uτ,uτ=one completed cell commit. \\tau_c(h)=N_c(h)\\,u_\\tau, \\qquad u_\\tau=\\text{one completed cell commit}. The unit is an event convention; geometry has not selected a duration for it. The source retains only bits, while the integers describe histories: Mimod2=biM_i\\bmod2=b_i and Ncmod2=pcN_c\\bmod2=p_c. A finite bit pattern therefore does not specify elapsed event counts. Put ρci=bi⊕pc\\rho_{ci}=b_i\\oplus p_c. Orient an incidence c→ic\\to i when ρci=0\\rho_{ci}=0, and i→ci\\to c when ρci=1\\rho_{ci}=1. The guards are exactly the sink condition and each bit flip reverses every incident edge. Theorem C1 (count profiles and admissibility). Every initialized source history satisfies 0≤Mi−Nc≤1(i∈c),Mi−Nc=bi⊕pc. 0\\le M_i-N_c\\le1\\quad(i\\in c), \\qquad M_i-N_c=b_i\\mathbin{\\oplus}p_c. \\tag{1} Every nonnegative integer profile satisfying (1)’s inequalities is reachable. More generally, two such profiles ordered coordinatewise are joined by a history that increments exactly their coordinate differences. The initialized system is deadlock-free. Proof. At an enabled preparation, each incident count is either MiM_i or Mi−1M_i-1. Its required parity agreement forces all to equal MiM_i; incrementing MiM_i changes each affected difference from zero to one. At an enabled commit, all incident differences are one; incrementing NcN_c changes them to zero. This proves the invariant, readiness formula and preservation of already-established readiness under preparations. For ordered compatible profiles, process levels k increasingly. At each level perform each still-required kth preparation before each still-required kth commit. Before the kth preparation at i, its count is k-1. Target compatibility requires all incident cell targets to be at least k-1; those lower-level commits have occurred, and current compatibility prevents their counts from exceeding k-1. The preparation is enabled. Before the kth commit at c, all required kth preparations have occurred, and current compatibility forces their counts to equal k. The commit is enabled. Each step adds one required occurrence, proving reachability and the ordered-profile asserti