s the first from zero and joins it to the second in exactly Bc−1B_c-1 events. The target count is k at both ends, so no target event occurs on this joining history. At its end all target vertices have count k+1; append CcC_c. Reapplying the bound after each prefix proves repeated local progress. ∎ Height form and relation to prior work. Set hi=2Mi−1h_i=2M_i-1 and hc=2Nch_c=2N_c. Adjacent heights differ by one; every edge points from higher to lower height, and firing a local minimum increases its height by two. For any target actor a, including a vertex actor, the exact worst-case bound is Ba=1+∑v∈V(G)distG(a,v). B_a=1+\\sum_{v\\in V(G)}\\operatorname{dist}_G(a,v). \\tag{3a} Indeed, while ha=H0h_a=H_0 is fixed, each height lies between H0−dG(a,v)H_0-d_G(a,v) and H0+dG(a,v)H_0+d_G(a,v). Each actor v can therefore fire at most dG(a,v)d_G(a,v) times. The two extreme height profiles are admissible: adjacent distances differ by exactly one in a bipartite graph. Choose H0H_0 with the target’s parity and large enough that both profiles correspond to nonnegative counts. C1 joins the lower profile to the upper profile with no target firing. At the upper profile the target is a sink; appending its firing attains (3a). For a cell target, dG(c,e)=2ded_G(c,e)=2d_e and dG(c,i)=2δi+1d_G(c,i)=2\\delta_i+1, so (3a) is exactly (3). This is the zero-circulation sector of the orientation lattice in [6]. With root a, Propp’s height convention is HP(v)=−(hv−ha)/2H_P(v)=-(h_v-h_a)/2, using edge weight 1/21/2. Its coordinate range is D(v)=dG(a,v)D(v)=d_G(a,v). Ordered height moves and the sum-of-height-differences argument (Propositions 15-16 on PDF page 12 and the proof of Proposition 24 on PDF page 18 in the cited version) give the target-avoiding total ∑vD(v)\\sum_vD(v). Formula (3a) adds the target firing. We have not established that this exact formula was previously printed verbatim; the derivation already follows from the existing framework. For one tetrahedron, Bc=5B_c=5: four prep