mentation and finite observation The following propositions separate source implementation, recording, acquisition and termination. Assume a finite connected tetrahedral carrier, unique register ownership, reliable capacity-one ports, and the local atomic actions of Appendices A-C. Source bits, acknowledgment and recorder word start at zero; ports start U, recorder control IDLE, and scan requests, frozen flags and result slots are empty. These are mathematical transition systems, not a fault-tolerant deployment specification. Proposition C4.1 (source implementation). With recorder and scan gates absent, the initialized port protocol is weakly bisimilar to the source under projection to actor bits, with Fire observable and port actions hidden. Its activity satisfies Tprot=∑i(3gi+1)Mi+13∑cNc+Q,0≤Q≤6J, T_{\\mathrm{prot}}=\\sum_i(3g_i+1)M_i+13\\sum_cN_c+Q, \\qquad0\\le Q\\le6J, \\tag{7} where gi=|I(i)|g_i=|I(i)|. Proof. Appendix A gives the transition rules, the certificate invariant, both directions of transition matching, and the port potential Q. ∎ Proposition C4.2 (bounded recorder). For width w≥1w\\ge1, the recorder preserves a logical record 0≤R≤2w−10\\le R\\le2^w-1 and Na=R+hN_a=R+h, where h=pa⊕α∈{0,1}h=p_a\\oplus\\alpha\\in\\{0,1\\}. The anchor cannot fire while h=1. Hiding carry steps and observing Ack realizes a bounded atomic recorder. Proof. Appendix B gives the abstraction in each control mode and proves its preservation; it also computes the complete carry and overflow costs. This claim does not assert bisimulation with an unrestricted source after observer gates are added. ∎ Proposition C4.3 (coherent acquisition). An admitted scan returns data determining counts at the declared cut sigma: Nc(σ)=R+h+ν*,c−ν*,a,Mi(σ)=R+h+μ*,i−ν*,a. N_c(\\sigma)=R+h+\\nu_{*,c}-\\nu_{*,a}, \\quad M_i(\\sigma)=R+h+\\mu_{*,i}-\\nu_{*,a}. \\tag{8} Proof. Appendix C holds the recorder stable and freezes all actors before the collection tour. Each collected bit equals its value at