 For example, after preparing all five vertices of two face-sharing tetrahedra, both cell commits are independently enabled: their total count is two and their commit-weighted causal depth is one. Indexed events make the source dependencies explicit. Each PikP_i^k requires every incident Cck−1C_c^{k-1}, with level zero supplied by initialization; each CckC_c^k requires every incident PikP_i^k. Their transitive closure gives a partial order. For a fixed compatible count profile, take all indexed occurrences up to its counts. This occurrence set is downward-closed: k≤Mik\\le M_i implies k−1≤Nck-1\\le N_c for every incident c, while k≤Nck\\le N_c implies k≤Mik\\le M_i for every incident i; level-zero requirements are supplied by initialization. Every legal history respects these dependencies. Conversely, induct on a linear extension: before PikP_i^k, its own count and every incident cell count are k−1k-1, since the prerequisite Cck−1C_c^{k-1} has occurred and CckC_c^k must wait for PikP_i^k; before CckC_c^k, every incident vertex count is k, since PikP_i^k has occurred and Pik+1P_i^{k+1} must wait for CckC_c^k. Each next event is therefore enabled, including k=1 by zero initialization. Thus legal histories of the profile are exactly these linear extensions. This source order does not determine the serialized execution or the communication history. Internal retriangulations are absent. An extension that treats them as zero-time changes must prove that the proposed history equivalence preserves both counts and continuation behavior. A pentachoron coordinate array likewise supplies neither a time covector nor a metric for raising indices. The substantive UD questions are therefore still the selection of a dynamics, a justified treatment of changing carriers, and an independently testable physical interpretation. The results here make the current counting convention explicit. Appendix A. Local protocol and activity accounting Give each vertex and cell one exclusive actor. Initialize all source bits to zero, ports to U and mailboxes empty. Every directed incidence request port has stage U (unsent), R (request pending), A (acknowledgment pending), or D (received). Send copies the requester’s bit and changes U to R. Reply changes R to A when the peer’s bit equals the request bit for a vertex requester, or differs for a cell requester. Receive changes A to D. Fire requires every own port to be D, flips the owned source bit and resets only those own ports to U, clearing their request payloads. This is one local atomic action over all owned collection modes; its cost is not one bit write. Send, Reply and Receive each operate one incident port/mailbox at the stated atomic boundary. Replies remain possible while the responder waits on its own requests. Local modes and mailboxes implement these stages with finite storage; no global atomic read is assumed. Every pending request holds its owner’s current bit because that owner cannot Fire before its own receipts complete. A successful certificate remains valid: a vertex Fire requires equality with all incident cells, whereas any neighboring cell certificate about that vertex would require inequality. Both cannot hold before the Fire. The symmetric argument applies to a cell Fire. Consequently a Fire cannot invalidate another actor’s successful certificate, and every Fire is source-admissible. Conversely, hold source bits fixed when a source operation at actor x is enabled. Each unfinished outgoing port needs at most Send, Reply and Receive before Fire, so the event lifts in at most 3deg(x)+13\\deg(x)+1 steps without another Fire. The formal matching argument below specifies the weak bisimulation on the initialized sectors when coordination steps are hidden. Requester aliases of a vertex preparation are identified; they are not extra occurrences. Transition system and matching proof. For each actor x store its bit sxs_x. For each directed request port (x,y)(x,y) store stage qxy∈{U,R,A,D}q_{xy}\\