os(\\omega s)], r(s)=ρ4+κ2cos(ωs)4+κ2,a(s)=12. r(s)=\\rho\\frac{4+\\kappa^2\\cos(\\omega s)}{4+\\kappa^2}, \\qquad a(s)=\\tfrac12. It satisfies f²+v²+r²=ρ². Thus observing cell energy alone would not distinguish the nonlinear conversion mechanism from ordinary seed-energy injection through this parent. A later observation proposal must separate the relevant energy stores and powers. Likewise, energy-only histories cannot distinguish ρ from −ρ. A handedness readout requires a separately defined signed or phase-sensitive observable and reference; the orientation convention alone is not a physical detector. 7. Why this respects the earlier no-go result The extended law is fully S4-equivariant. A nonzero prepared ρ, however, is reversed by an odd permutation that fixes the packet vertex launch. The full initial state is therefore not reflection-invariant. The prior uniqueness/isotropy theorem does not require its cell amplitude to remain zero. If the full initial state is reflection-symmetric (ρ=0 here, with no other odd seed), the earlier obstruction returns exactly. This model transports an explicitly supplied distinction; it does not generate handedness from exact symmetric zero or from numerical noise. Orientation signs retain both arithmetic forms: for inversion count N, N=2q+r and N≡r (mod 2), r∈{0,1}; the two-position clock reads r, and the sign is (−1)^r. The modulus 2 is prime, while 24=2³·3 and 12=2²·3 describe the index-two group split. These finite factors do not determine λ, κ, or ρ. 8. Closure status and source obligations Item Status Reservoir coordinate, representation, energy and reciprocal dynamics Explicitly defined candidate parent Allowed constant port space Exactly classified as span(b,z) Port compatibility with nonzero feedback Boundary-face port selected within this ansatz by Jacobi Energy exchange and donor response Exactly closed within the model Source of nonzero prepared ρ Open; moving the seed to a parent does not derive it Source or externa