ne. Every basis matrix is exported in the result file; reflection-even/odd and locality-restricted bases are retained separately. The adjacent-grade restriction permits pairs of coordinates whose grades differ by one, even if their simplices are not incident. Original Hasse support is stricter: it permits only the existing nonzero entries of A. These conditions must not be interchanged. The adjacent-grade cell obstruction has a precise reason. Under A4 the three-dimensional invariant sector consists of the uniform vertex direction, the oriented boundary-face direction, and the cell. All A4-equivariant operators preserve that sector and its orthogonal complement. Adjacent-grade operators annihilate the uniform vertex direction. A pure vertex’s component in this sector therefore cannot enter the face or cell directions, and its complementary component cannot reach the sector. Thus merely relaxing reflection symmetry while preserving grade adjacency is insufficient for the cell route. 3. A rotation-invariant candidate without an incidence selector Define three orthonormal vectors on the existing carrier: h: the uniform vertex vector, with four entries 1/2 in grade 0. b: the oriented boundary-face vector, with grade-2 block B3/2. z: the unit cell vector in grade 3. Under an even permutation all three are invariant. Under an odd permutation h is invariant while b and z change sign. This is a representation statement about oriented amplitudes, not proof that a material packet has physical chirality. Set K=bhT−hbT,Aχ=A+χK, K=bh^T-hb^T,\\qquad A_\\chi=A+\\chi K, with fixed real symbolic χ. This rank-two skew perturbation couples the uniform vertex mode to the boundary-face mode. It treats all vertices and faces covariantly under proper rotations; no edge or face is selected. For an oriented simplex action Qπ, QπKQπT=sgn(π)K,QπAχQπT=Asgn(π)χ. Q_\\pi KQ_\\pi^T=\\operatorname{sgn}(\\pi)K, \\quad Q_\\pi A_\\chi Q_\\pi^T=A_{\\operatorname{sgn}(\\pi)\\chi}. A nonzero fixed χ ther