e a nontrivial distinction. This fails the manuscript’s non-collapse requirement. Ruling: excluded under the declared distinction requirement. 3.2 Two-role countermodel Take V={0,1}V=\\{0,1\\}, with complement ¬0=1\\neg 0=1, ¬1=0\\neg 1=0, and let the relation be inequality. Closure is imposed as a property of the algebra: ¬(¬x)=x. \\neg(\\neg x)=x. Here content and contrast are carrier values, mediation is the inequality/complement operation, and closure is algebraic involution rather than a fourth site. This does not satisfy a demand that every function occupy a distinct position, but that demand is precisely the missing premise. Ruling: countermodel to G2 unless functional roles are axiomatically required to be carrier-distinct. 3.3 Three-role countermodel Take V={A,¬A,R}V=\\{A,\\neg A,R\\}. Let RR mediate AA and ¬A\\neg A, and define closure as the global property that all admissible evaluations and transformations are generated internally by RR and preserve the distinction. Closure is then a predicate on the realized structure, not an additional vertex. This is a valid model of four functions distributed over three carrier positions whenever a role may be higher-order or self-applicative. Ruling: countermodel to G2 unless the theory forbids a mediator from also implementing closure. 3.4 Four-role incomplete-coupling countermodel Let V={0,1,2,3}V=\\{0,1,2,3\\} and E={01,12,23,30}. E=\\{01,12,23,30\\}. This is C4C_4. It is: reciprocal; connected; vertex-transitive, hence non-privileged at the vertex level; internally mediated by paths; incomplete, with four rather than six edges. Therefore non-privilege, reciprocity, and connectedness do not force complete pairwise coupling. An exhaustive enumeration of simple graphs on four vertices gives four vertex-transitive isomorphism classes: Graph Edges Connected Automorphism order empty graph 0 no 24 two disjoint edges 2K22K_2 2 no 8 four-cycle C4C_4 4 yes 8 complete graph K4K_4 6 yes 24 Connectedness removes the first t