oxed{28=14_{\\mathrm tree}+14_{\\mathrm cycle/heldout}.} AUTO_LOCK NONEMPTY_SIMPLEX_HASSE_FACTOR_THEOREM_v0.1 II. Tetrahedral 14/14 split is simplex-unique For any nondegenerate simplex with n≥2n\\ge2, the spanning-tree and non-tree counts are equal iff Tn=Cn. T_n=C_n. Using the common source factor 2n−1−1>02^{n-1}-1>0, 2(2n−1−1)=(n−2)(2n−1−1) 2(2^{n-1}-1) = (n-2)(2^{n-1}-1) iff 2=n−2, 2=n-2, so n=4. \\boxed{n=4.} Thus among nondegenerate simplex face Hasse graphs, the tetrahedron is the unique simplex for which a spanning-tree PSR reconstruction uses exactly half of all Hasse incidences and leaves exactly half held out. AUTO_LOCK TETRAHEDRON_UNIQUE_EQUAL_PSR_TREE_AND_HELDOUT_SIMPLEX_THEOREM_v0.1 This is a combinatorial theorem. It is not a physical uniqueness theorem. III. The typed fourteen collision cluster Four distinct objects equal 1414 on the filled tetrahedron: Hodge non-harmonic chain dimension 15−1=14; 15-1=14; Hasse spanning-tree incidence count T4=15−1=14; T_4=15-1=14; Hasse cycle rank / held-out incidence count 28−15+1=14; 28-15+1=14; continuous degrees of freedom in normalized positive support coordinates (ρ1,…,ρ15),∑ρi=1⇒14. (\\rho_1,\\ldots,\\rho_{15}),\\quad \\sum\\rho_i=1 \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad 14. The first two both involve subtracting one but from different mathematical objects. The third is a graph-cycle count. The fourth is a simplex dimension of a normalized probability/support vector. Therefore 14nonharmonic≠14tree≠14Hassecycle≠14normalizedsupportDOF \\boxed{ 14_{\\mathrm nonharmonic} \\neq 14_{\\mathrm tree} \\neq 14_{\\mathrm Hasse\\ cycle} \\neq 14_{\\mathrm normalized\\ support\\ DOF} } as typed objects, despite numerical equality. AUTO_LOCK FOURTEEN_TYPED_COLLISION_CLUSTER_v0.1 AUTO_FREEZE EQUAL_FOURTEEN_COUNTS_IMPLY_SAME_UD_OBJECT. IV. Hasse incidence count from the f-vector For a tetrahedral 3-complex with f-vector (f0,f1,f2,f3), (f_0,f_1,f_2,f_3), the simplex Hasse incidence count is exactly EH=2f1+3f2+4f3. \\boxed{ E_H = 2f_1+3f_2+