mp 1 -> Tanks 1 and 4; Pump 2 -> Tanks 2 and 3. SOURCE_BLOCKED: no searched public author/manufacturer source binds InTaSet M1/M2 to explicit (gamma_1,gamma_2) values. SOURCE_BLOCKED: no searched public author/manufacturer source supplies a physical command/speed -> delivered-flow calibration for the custom Lehigh testbed. AUTO_FREEZE: numerical-model values of gamma_i and k_i may not be silently transferred to InTaSet physical records. DERIVED / EXACT CONDITIONAL: for source-certified delivered flow changes a = Delta q_1, b = Delta q_2, the six-edge source family is exact. DERIVED / EXACT: the edge pair (14,23) is split-invariant: G_14 = (a-b)/4, G_23 = -(a-b)/4, independent of gamma_1,gamma_2. This split invariance does not remove the pump-flow calibration requirement. report/UD_INTASET_M1M2_AND_PUMP_CALIBRATION_SOURCE_AUDIT_REPORT_v0.1.mdUD InTaSet M1/M2 and Pump Calibration Source Audit Report v0.1 Executive ruling Two source-side gaps were audited in parallel. Neither is closed numerically by the currently public source-authored corpus. The common QTP routing family is source-authored and exact, but InTaSet M1 / M2 are not publicly cross-walked to explicit (gamma_1,gamma_2) values in the searched sources. The physical Lehigh/Bitlismen platform is pump-actuated and reconfigurable, but no searched public source provides the InTaSet physical pump command/speed -> delivered-flow calibration needed to transform workbook commands into Delta q. No response fitting was used to repair either source gap. What closed The source-authored QTP support topology is Pump 1 -> Tanks 1 and 4; Pump 2 -> Tanks 2 and 3. Therefore, for source-certified valve splits and delivered pump-flow changes, RQTP(γ1,γ2)=(γ100γ201−γ21−γ10) R_{QTP}(\\gamma_1,\\gamma_2)= \\begin{pmatrix} \\gamma_1&0\\\\ 0&\\gamma_2\\\\ 0&1-\\gamma_2\\\\ 1-\\gamma_1&0 \\end{pmatrix} and GEext=12CTP⊥RQTPΔq G_E^{ext}=\\frac12C^TP_\\perp R_{QTP}\\Delta q are frozen exactly. New exact split-invariant result In edge order (12,13,14,23,24,34), G14=Δq1−Δq24,G23=−G14. G_{14}=\\frac{\\Delta q_1-\\Delta q_2}{4}, \\qquad G_{23}=-G_{14}. This subchannel does not depend on gamma_1 or gamma_2. That is a genuine source simplification: once physical delivered pump-flow changes are known, one exact six-edge contrast component can be computed without knowing the valve split configuration. It does not authorize use of raw pump commands as physical flows. M1/M2 source audit The authenticated uploaded archive contains four 4T M1/M2 training/testing workbooks and no accompanying configuration sheet. Workbook metadata exposes no author-authored valve split or pump routing table. The public InTaSet repository states that four testbed configurations exist but does not define M1/M2 through a gamma crosswalk on the searched public surface. A separate overlapping Lehigh QTP publication gives numerical-model MP and NMP parameter examples. Those are useful QTP references but are not an InTaSet configuration receipt. Ruling M1 -> MP, M2 -> NMP, either reverse assignment, and direct gamma-number assignment remain source-blocked. Pump calibration source audit The physical platform is a custom/reconfigurable Bitlismen quadruple-tank installation at Lehigh. Public manufacturer material states that the platform has two independently controlled pumps, programmable tank flow, solenoid-controlled pump inlets, pressure-sensor tank levels, and modifiable couplings. No searched public source gives the calibration needed to evaluate qi=Φi(ui,ζ;c) q_i=\\Phi_i(u_i,\\zeta;c) from the InTaSet Pump i fields. The Water Research QTP equations contain linear k_i u_i terms in the numerical model. These coefficients are not a physical calibration receipt for the custom Lehigh hardware. Ruling Physical Delta q_i remains source-blocked from raw InTaSet Pump 1/2 commands. Governance consequences AUTO_LOCK common QTP support topology; parametric QTP flow-incidence family; exact six-edge source family conditional on delivered flow; 