neric leak tolerance is embedded. Current execution status: SOURCE_BLOCKED. Physical promotion: 0. manuscript/UD_JOP_v2.40_LEHIGH_ISOLATION_LEAK_QUALIFICATION_ADDENDUM_v0.1.mdJOP v2.40 Addendum — Lehigh Isolation / Leak Qualification Gate v0.1 The prospective Lehigh tank-side physical comparator is supplemented by a pretarget hydraulic isolation qualification gate. The Bitlismen source confirms manual valves on all tank outlets, independently controlled pumps, pump-inlet solenoids, and four tank-level pressure sensors. These control states do not by themselves prove zero residual flow. For each of the two future comparator states, a matched-level no-pump hold is therefore required. Using source-authored tank geometry Vi(hi)V_i(h_i), the no-pump drift is estimated as the OLS volume slope ℓ̂i,r=∑n(tn−t‾)(Vi(hi,n)−V‾i)∑n(tn−t‾)2. \\hat\\ell_{i,r} = \\frac{\\sum_n(t_n-\\bar t)(V_i(h_{i,n})-\\bar V_i)} {\\sum_n(t_n-\\bar t)^2}. Each tank/state must be statistically or deterministically compatible with zero under a pretarget source-authored metrology band. Detectable drift blocks comparator scoring. A passed no-drift test is not promoted to exact zero leakage. The maximum undetected leak is retained as Li,r=|ℓ̂i,r|+frozen drift band, L_{i,r}=|\\hat\\ell_{i,r}|+\\text{frozen drift band}, and contributes conservatively BR14leak=14∑i,rLi,r B_{R_{14}}^{\\mathrm leak} = \\frac14\\sum_{i,r}L_{i,r} to the final Lehigh comparator tolerance. Thus imperfect manual-valve closure cannot silently enter the physical comparator as an unmodeled assumption. Physical promotion remains zero. protocol/UD_LEHIGH_ISOLATION_LEAK_QUALIFICATION_PREREG_v0.1.mdUD Lehigh Isolation / Leak Qualification Preregistration v0.1 Generated: 2026-09-09T02:26:42.678196+00:00 Status: PRETARGET_FROZEN / METROLOGY + HARDWARE RECEIPTS PENDING Claim class: PROSPECTIVE_SOURCE-AUTHORED_ISOLATION_QUALIFICATION_PROTOCOL Physical promotion: 0 0. Purpose Qualify the actual Lehigh/Bitlismen hydraulic isolation before the preregistered tank-side physical comparator is allowed to score. The comparator assumes that, during a fresh filling window, tank storage change is attributable to delivered pump inflow on the declared branch. Manual valve closure and pump-off commands cannot silently be equated with zero residual hydraulic flow. This protocol therefore measures the residual no-pump storage drift directly. 1. Source-authored hardware basis The Bitlismen Quadruple Tank Process Control Research Platform source states that: the system contains two coupled-tank subsystems; the upper-tank bottom outlet can feed the lower tank; lower tanks drain to the main reservoir; upper tanks have another direct outlet to the reservoir; all outlets have manual valves; each tank has a pressure sensor for level measurement; couplings may be changed using 3/8″3/8'', 5/16″5/16'', and 1/4″1/4'' tubes; water is delivered by two independently controlled pumps; pump inlet flow can be opened/closed using solenoid valves. Bitlismen separately identifies the April 2024 Lehigh installation as a custom quadruple-tank platform. These source statements establish that hydraulic isolation is physically configurable. They do not establish that a commanded/manual CLOSED state has zero leakage. 2. Qualification principle For each future comparator state r∈{0,1}, r\\in\\{0,1\\}, perform a matched-level no-pump hold before the fresh target score is opened. At the matched state: bring all four tanks to the predeclared state-rr level band; set both pump drives to the source-declared OFF state; close both pump-inlet solenoid paths; set every non-pump outlet/intertank/bypass path required by the comparator to the declared CLOSED/isolation state; wait the predeclared settling interval; record a fixed, predeclared hold window; do not alter the hold window after observing drift. The same tank geometry and level-metrology receipts required by the comparator are used here. 3. General storage-drift estimator For tank ii, use the source-authored geometry Vi=Vi(hi). V_i=V_i(h_i). For each hold-window level sample, transform vi,n=Vi(hi,n). v_{i,n}=V_i(h_{i,n}). Let the frozen hold window have sample times tnt_n. Define the OLS-with-intercept volume slope ℓ̂i,r=∑n(tn−t‾)(vi,n−v‾i)∑n(tn−t‾)2. \\boxed{ \\hat\\ell_{i,r} = \\frac{ \\sum_n(t_n-\\bar t)(v_{i,n}-\\bar v_i) }{ \\sum_n(t_n-\\bar t)^2 }. } Units: [ℓ̂i,r]=volume/time. [\\hat\\ell_{i,r}]={\\mathrm volume/time}. Interpretation: ℓ̂i,r>0 \\hat\\ell_{i,r}>0 means net unmeasured inflow/storage gain, while ℓ̂i,r<0 \\hat\\ell_{i,r}<0 means net unmeasured outflow/storage loss. Both signs are isolation failures when detectably nonzero. Constant-area specialization Only if source-authored geometry supplies constant area AiA_i, Vi=Aihi+Vi,0, V_i=A_i h_i+V_{i,0}, then ℓ̂i,r=Aim̂i,rhold. \\boxed{ \\hat\\ell_{i,r}=A_i\\hat m_{i,r}^{\\mathrm hold}. } No generic geometry is admitted. 4. Matched-level requirement Leakage through imperfect valves can depend on hydrostatic head. One no-pump test at an unrelated level is therefore insufficient. For each comparator state rr, the hold test must be performed at levels matched to that state. Define the hold and comparator mean levels h‾i,rhold,h‾i,rfill. \\bar h_{i,r}^{\\mathrm hold}, \\qquad \\bar h_{i,r}^{\\mathrm fill}. The matched-level gate is not assigned an arbitrary tolerance. It passes only if the authenticated level metrology establishes compatibility: Hard-bound route |h‾i,rhold−h‾i,rfill|≤BΔhi,rmatch. \\boxed{ |\\bar h_{i,r}^{\\mathrm hold}-\\bar h_{i,r}^{\\mathrm fill}| \\le B_{\\Delta h_{i,r}}^{\\mathrm match}. } Standard-uncertainty route |h‾i,rhold−h‾i,rfill|≤kmatchu(Δhi,rmatch). \\boxed{ |\\bar h_{i,r}^{\\mathrm hold}-\\bar h_{i,r}^{\\mathrm fill}| \\le k_{\\mathrm match}\\, u(\\Delta h_{i,r}^{\\mathrm match}). } Both BΔhmatchB_{\\Delta h}^{\\mathrm match} or kmatchk_{\\mathrm match} must be frozen from source/metrology before target scoring. No post-hoc level-match tolerance is allowed. 5. Zero-drift metrology gate The no-pump isolation test uses the same two allowed metrology routes as the comparator. Route H — hard error bound Let Bℓi,r B_{\\ell_{i,r}} be the authenticated propagated absolute bound on the hold-slope estimate. Tank ii, state rr qualifies only if |ℓ̂i,r|≤Bℓi,r. \\boxed{ |\\hat\\ell_{i,r}| \\le B_{\\ell_{i,r}}. } Route C — standard covariance Let u(ℓi,r) u(\\ell_{i,r}) be the source-propagated standard uncertainty and let khold k_{\\mathrm hold} be a pretarget coverage factor. Tank ii, state rr qualifies only if |ℓ̂i,r|≤kholdu(ℓi,r). \\boxed{ |\\hat\\ell_{i,r}| \\le k_{\\mathrm hold}u(\\ell_{i,r}). } No value of kholdk_{\\mathrm hold} is set in this protocol. 6. OLS hold-slope uncertainty For fixed sample times, define wn=tn−t‾Stt,Stt=∑n(tn−t‾)2. w_n = \\frac{t_n-\\bar t}{S_{tt}}, \\qquad S_{tt}=\\sum_n(t_n-\\bar t)^2. Then ℓ̂i,r=∑nwnvi,n. \\hat\\ell_{i,r} = \\sum_n w_n v_{i,n}. For a source-authored volume-sample covariance matrix ΣV,i,r\\Sigma_{V,i,r}, u2(ℓi,r)=wTΣV,i,rw \\boxed{ u^2(\\ell_{i,r}) = w^T\\Sigma_{V,i,r}w } is exact for fixed timing. If timing uncertainty is material, the already frozen slope timing-sensitivity law must be included; it cannot be silently ignored. For hard volume-sample bounds BV,i,nB_{V,i,n}, Bℓi,r≤∑n|wn|BV,i,n+Bℓi,rtiming \\boxed{ B_{\\ell_{i,r}} \\le \\sum_n |w_n| B_{V,i,n} + B_{\\ell_{i,r}}^{\\mathrm timing} } with the timing term source-derived or explicitly proven negligible. 7. Qualification is not a zero-leak claim Even when |ℓ̂i,r| |\\hat\\ell_{i,r}| lies inside its no-drift band, the actual residual leak is not set to zero. Define a conservative undetected leak allowance. Hard-bound route Li,r=|ℓ̂i,r|+Bℓi,r. \\boxed{ L_{i,r} = |\\hat\\ell_{i,r}|+B_{\\ell_{i,r}}. } Standard-uncertainty route Li,r=|ℓ̂i,r|+kholdu(ℓi,r). \\boxed{ L_{i,r} = |\\hat\\ell_{i,r}|+k_{\\mathrm hold}u(\\ell_{i,r}). } This quantity is carried forward as a bounded contamination term. 8. Leak contribution to the Lehigh R14R_{14} comparator Let the signed residual non-pump storage drifts during the matched fill states be λi,r. \\lambda_{i,r}. The contamination of the two-state branch-contrast residual is R14leak=14[(λ1,1+λ4,1−λ1,0−λ4,0)−(λ2,1+λ3,1−λ2,0−λ3,0)]. \\boxed{ R_{14}^{\\mathrm leak} = \\frac14 \\left[ (\\lambda_{1,1}+\\lambda_{4,1}-\\lambda_{1,0}-\\lambda_{4,0}) - (\\lambda_{2,1}+\\lambda_{3,1}-\\lambda_{2,0}-\\lambda_{3,0}) \\right]. } If each matched-state drift obeys |λi,r|≤Li,r, |\\lambda_{i,r}|\\le L_{i,r}, then by the triangle inequality, BR14leak=14∑r∈{0,1}∑i=14Li,r. \\boxed{ B_{R_{14}}^{\\mathrm leak} = \\frac14 \\sum_{r\\in\\{0,1\\}} \\sum_{i=1}^4 L_{i,r}. } This is a conservative bound. No cancellation between tank leaks is assumed. 9. Integration with the main comparator gate Let the main comparator’s pre-leak metrology band be: Hard-bound comparator BR14meas. B_{R_{14}}^{\\mathrm meas}. Then the final allowed band becomes BR14total=BR14meas+BR14leak. \\boxed{ B_{R_{14}}^{\\mathrm total} = B_{R_{14}}^{\\mathrm meas} + B_{R_{14}}^{\\mathrm leak}. } The physical comparator PASS condition is |R14(LEHIGH)|≤BR14total. \\boxed{ |R_{14}^{(\\mathrm{LEHIGH})}| \\le B_{R_{14}}^{\\mathrm total}. } Standard-uncertainty comparator If the ordinary measurement contribution is kcovu(R14), k_{\\mathrm cov}u(R_{14}), the bounded isolation nuisance is added conservatively: TR14total=kcovu(R14)+BR14leak. \\boxed{ T_{R_{14}}^{\\mathrm total} = k_{\\mathrm cov}u(R_{14}) + B_{R_{14}}^{\\mathrm leak}. } PASS requires |R14(LEHIGH)|≤TR14total. \\boxed{ |R_{14}^{(\\mathrm{LEHIGH})}| \\le T_{R_{14}}^{\\mathrm total}. } The leak allowance is not placed in quadrature unless a future source establishes a probabilistic leak model and a separately frozen version permits it. 10. Isolation failure rule If any tank/state fails the no-drift metrology gate, |ℓ̂i,r|>frozen zero-drift band, |\\hat\\ell_{i,r}|>\\text{frozen zero-drift band}, then: ISOLATION_QUALIFICATION := FAIL LEHIGH_PHYSICAL_COMPARATOR := NOT_SCORED v0.1 does not repair a detected leak by subtracting the observed hold drift. Why: leak may be head-dependent; leak may change between hold and fill; manual-valve hysteresis/reseating may matter; subtraction would introduce an additional constitutive assumption. A future leak-correction protocol would require independent source derivation and preregistration. 11. Pump-off / valve-state source receipt The hold test requires a pretarget operator/hardware receipt containing: pump1_drive_state := OFF pump2_drive_state := OFF pump1_inlet_solenoid := CLOSED pump2_inlet_solenoid := CLOSED tank1_to_reservoir_path := CLOSED tank2_to_reservoir_path := CLOSED tank3_to_tank1_path := CLOSED tank4_to_tank2_path := CLOSED tank3_direct_reservoir_path := CLOSED tank4_direct_reservoir_path := CLOSED all_other_bypass_paths := NONE_OR_CLOSED overflow_possible := FALSE If the custom Lehigh build uses different path names, the source operator must supply an equivalent typed map. Unknown path: SOURCE_BLOCKED_CONFIGURATION 12. Hold-window controls Before fresh target acquisition, freeze: state0 hold target level band state1 hold target level band settling duration hold duration sample interval OLS estimator geometry receipt level metrology receipt timing metrology receipt k_hold or hard-bound route k_match or hard matched-level bound No hold window may be lengthened because the initial result is inconvenient. No drift segment may be dropped. 13. Optional repeated-hold diagnostic A repeated hold may be preregistered before target exposure to test manual-valve reseating repeatability. If used, the repeat count and aggregation rule must be frozen beforehand. v0.1 does not require a repeat and does not authorize adding repeats after seeing the first drift. 14. Current source disposition At freeze time: MANUAL_OUTLET_VALVES := SOURCE_CONFIRMED PUMP_INLET_SOLENOIDS := SOURCE_CONFIRMED TANK_LEVEL_SENSORS := SOURCE_CONFIRMED CUSTOM_LEHIGH_PLATFORM := SOURCE_CONFIRMED RUN_SPECIFIC_VALVE_STATE :=