s the generalized physical action-mode intertwiner. It reproduces the algebraic forms of Q282/Q285 without assuming physical identity. 3. Metric governance The action scale depends on the physical-to-UD amplitude metric GG. Under a coordinate change q=Tq′, q=Tq', the objects must transform together: M′=T𝖳MT, M' = T^\\mathsf TMT, K′=T𝖳KT, K' = T^\\mathsf TKT, G′=T𝖳GT. G' = T^\\mathsf TGT. If all three are transformed consistently, 𝒜r\\mathcal A_r is representation-invariant. But choosing a new GG after target exposure changes the physical amplitude definition. Therefore: coordinate choice is harmless;post-target metric choice is not. \\boxed{ \\text{coordinate choice is harmless;} \\quad \\text{post-target metric choice is not.} } For the Khezri source we freeze the paper’s canonical transformed phase coordinates and use G=I2 \\boxed{ G=I_2 } as the declared auxiliary Euclidean active-amplitude map. Any alternative metric requires a separately preregistered intertwiner. 4. Source-fitted parameters The paper’s two-dimensional fitted values are Iz=242±3nA, I_z = 242\\pm3\\ {\\mathrm nA}, Csh=62±1fF, C_{\\mathrm sh} = 62\\pm1\\ {\\mathrm fF}, Cz=4.85±0.07fF, C_z = 4.85\\pm0.07\\ {\\mathrm fF}, α=0.423±0.001, \\alpha = 0.423\\pm0.001, with fixed asymmetry d=0.102. d=0.102. Therefore D=2Csh+(4α+1)Cz D = 2C_{\\mathrm sh} + (4\\alpha+1)C_z gives D=137.0562fF. \\boxed{ D = 137.0562\\ {\\mathrm fF}. } Assuming the source-reported one-sigma fit uncertainties are independent for this propagation, u(D)=2.00895fF. \\boxed{ u(D) = 2.00895\\ {\\mathrm fF}. } 5. Numeric physical mass metrics Using exact SI ϕ0=ℏ/(2e)\\phi_0=\\hbar/(2e): Fast transformed coordinate Mfast=2ϕ02Cz. M_{\\mathrm fast} = 2\\phi_0^2C_z. Numerically, Mfast=1.05061423×10−45 \\boxed{ M_{\\mathrm fast} = 1.05061423\\times10^{-45} } in the action-per-angular-frequency mass dimension, with u(Mfast)=1.51635×10−47. \\boxed{ u(M_{\\mathrm fast}) = 1.51635\\times10^{-47}. } Relative uncertainty: 1.4433%. 1.4433\\%. Slow transformed coordinate — 2D fit Mslow(2D)=2ϕ02D. M_{\\mathrm slow}^{(2D)} = 2\\phi_0^2D. Numerically, Mslow(2D)=2.96893183×10−44 \\boxed{ M_{\\mathrm slow}^{(2D)} = 2.96893183\\times10^{-44} } with u(Mslow(2D))=4.35182×10−46. \\boxed{ u(M_{\\mathrm slow}^{(2D)}) = 4.35182\\times10^{-46}. } Relative uncertainty: 1.4658%. 1.4658\\%. 6. One-dimensional Born-Oppenheimer control The source reduces the slow branch to K1D=e22Cshn2. K_{1D} = \\frac{e^2}{2C_{\\mathrm sh}} n^2. The equivalent physical phase-coordinate capacitance is Ceff(1D)=4Csh. C_{\\mathrm eff}^{(1D)} = 4C_{\\mathrm sh}. Using the independently fitted one-dimensional value Csh(1D)=70±1fF, C_{\\mathrm sh}^{(1D)} = 70\\pm1\\ {\\mathrm fF}, the slow-mode physical mass is Mslow(1D)=4ϕ02Csh(1D) \\boxed{ M_{\\mathrm slow}^{(1D)} = 4\\phi_0^2C_{\\mathrm sh}^{(1D)} } or numerically Mslow(1D)=3.03270086×10−44 \\boxed{ M_{\\mathrm slow}^{(1D)} = 3.03270086\\times10^{-44} } with u(Mslow(1D))=4.33243×10−46. \\boxed{ u(M_{\\mathrm slow}^{(1D)}) = 4.33243\\times10^{-46}. } The ratio is Mslow(1D)Mslow(2D)=1.02147878. \\boxed{ \\frac{ M_{\\mathrm slow}^{(1D)} }{ M_{\\mathrm slow}^{(2D)} } = 1.02147878. } The difference is approximately 1.04σ \\boxed{ 1.04\\sigma } under independent uncertainty propagation. This is a source-level consistency check between the two fitted models. 7. Frequency-to-action transfer functions For any independently measured small-signal mode frequency fr=ωr2π, f_r = \\frac{\\omega_r}{2\\pi}, the dimensionless physical action ratio is ηr=Mrωrℏ. \\eta_r = \\frac{ M_r\\omega_r }{\\hbar}. Thus the fitted mass metrics define target-independent transfer functions. With ff in GHz: ηfast(2D)=(0.0625961±0.0009034)fGHz \\boxed{ \\eta_{\\mathrm fast}^{(2D)} = (0.0625961\\pm0.0009034) f_{\\mathrm GHz} } ηslow(2D)=(1.76890±0.02593)fGHz \\boxed{ \\eta_{\\mathrm slow}^{(2D)} = (1.76890\\pm0.02593) f_{\\mathrm GHz} } ηslow(1D)=(1.80690±0.02581)fGHz. \\boxed{ \\eta_{\\mathrm slow}^{(1D)} = (1.80690\\pm0.02581) 