dots\\}. Let λi(t)≥0 \\lambda_i(t)\\ge0 be the predictable event intensity. Then 𝔼[dNi(t)∣ℱt−]=λi(t)dt. \\boxed{ \\mathbb E[dN_i(t)\\mid\\mathcal F_{t^-}] = \\lambda_i(t)\\,dt. } The cumulative intensity is Λi(T)=∫0Tλi(t)dt. \\boxed{ \\Lambda_i(T) = \\int_0^T\\lambda_i(t)\\,dt. } For an inhomogeneous Poisson process, 𝔼[Ni(T)]=Λi(T). \\boxed{ \\mathbb E[N_i(T)] = \\Lambda_i(T). } In general, Λi(T)∉ℕ0. \\Lambda_i(T) \\notin \\mathbb N_0. Thus a smooth rate integral is naturally an expected count / compensator, not the realized integer event count. 3. Exact-count branch The historical wording can remain literally exact only if Γidt \\Gamma_i\\,dt is interpreted as the counting measure itself: dNi \\boxed{ dN_i } rather than a smooth ordinary function times dtdt. Then Ni(T)=∫0TdNi \\boxed{ N_i(T) = \\int_0^T dN_i } is exactly integer. But in that interpretation the amplitude ai(t)a_i(t) is fundamentally a jump/staircase process at event resolution, and ȧi \\dot a_i is distributional rather than an ordinary smooth derivative. The later slow-genesis smooth action is then a coarse-grained limit, not the same microscopic path object. 4. Smooth Poisson-intensity branch To preserve the historical slow-genesis language as an ordinary smooth trajectory, define Γi(t)=λi(t) \\boxed{ \\Gamma_i(t) = \\lambda_i(t) } as the Poisson intensity. Then the historical continuum object becomes Λgen(T)=∫0T∑iΓi(t)dt \\boxed{ \\Lambda_{\\mathrm gen}(T) = \\int_0^T\\sum_i\\Gamma_i(t)\\,dt } with Λgen=𝔼[Ngenreal] \\boxed{ \\Lambda_{\\mathrm gen} = \\mathbb E[N_{\\mathrm gen}^{\\mathrm real}] } under the Poisson model. The corresponding continuum action is Sgenesismean=ℏdΛgen. \\boxed{ S_{\\mathrm genesis}^{\\mathrm mean} = \\hbar_d\\Lambda_{\\mathrm gen}. } The realized microscopic action is instead Sgenesisreal=ℏdNgenreal. \\boxed{ S_{\\mathrm genesis}^{\\mathrm real} = \\hbar_d N_{\\mathrm gen}^{\\mathrm real}. } Therefore 𝔼[Sgenesisreal]=Sgenesismean. \\boxed{ \\mathbb E[S_{\\mathrm genesis}