    "content": "Derives a Gaussian coherence functional and temporal admission scale. The reported late autonomous drift is superseded by the later source-schedule repair. Governed release package · 0.1Triadic Coherence and Temporal AdmissionDerives a Gaussian coherence functional and temporal admission scale. The reported late autonomous drift is superseded by the later source-schedule repair.StatusFUNCTIONAL DERIVED / LATER SCHEDULE REPAIRPhysical promotion0Package files14Hash manifestVerified · 13 filesDownload immutable release ZIP ↓Package compositionpackage root14Show all 14 preserved pathsSOURCE_v07_genesis_sim_v1.pyUD_AUTONOMOUS_PHI_CLOSURE_RESULTS_v0.1.jsonUD_AUTONOMOUS_TRIADIC_COHERENCE_GAUSSIAN_CLOSURE_v0.1.mdUD_MANUSCRIPT_ADDENDUM_PHI_TEMPORAL_v0.1.mdUD_PHI_GAUSSIAN_EMPIRICAL_MOMENT_DIAGNOSTIC_v0.1.jsonUD_PHI_GAUSSIAN_HELDOUT_v0.1.jsonUD_PHI_TEMPORAL_MANIFEST_v0.1.jsonUD_PHI_TEMPORAL_REPRODUCER_v0.1.pyUD_PHI_TEMPORAL_RULE_FREEZE_v0.1.jsonUD_PHI_TEMPORAL_STATUS_v0.1.jsonUD_TEMPORAL_CLUSTER_ADMISSION_RESULTS_v0.1.jsonUD_TEMPORAL_CLUSTER_HELDOUT_v0.1.jsonUD_TEMPORAL_COHERENCE_CLUSTER_ADMISSION_v0.1.mdUD_TEMPORAL_TRACKING_ONE_TO_ONE_v0.1.jsonUD_AUTONOMOUS_TRIADIC_COHERENCE_GAUSSIAN_CLOSURE_v0.1.mdUD_AUTONOMOUS_TRIADIC_COHERENCE_GAUSSIAN_CLOSURE_v0.1 Date: 2026-09-13 Purpose: close the historical triadic-coherence functional Φ\\Phi from the six-variable current/previous triangle Gaussian law Status: EXACT_GAUSSIAN_FUNCTIONAL__HELDOUT_SIMULATION_PASS__FULL_SECOND_MOMENT_AUTONOMY_FAILS_LATE Physical promotion: 0 1. Historical source object For one oriented triangle let x=(aiajak),y=(ai−aj−ak−), x= \\begin{pmatrix} a_i\\\\a_j\\\\a_k \\end{pmatrix}, \\qquad y= \\begin{pmatrix} a_i^-\\\\a_j^-\\\\a_k^- \\end{pmatrix}, where (−)(-) denotes the previous source step. The historical simulator computes Φ△=aiaj−+ajak−+akai−ai2+aj2+ak2+ϵΦ. \\Phi_\\triangle = \\frac{ a_i a_j^-+ a_j a_k^-+ a_k a_i^- }{ a_i^2+a_j^2+a_k^2+\\epsilon_\\Phi }. Define the cyclic permutation matrix R=(010001100). R= \\begin{pmatrix} 0&1&0\\\\ 0&0&1\\\\ 1&0&0 \\end{pmatrix}. Then Φ△=xTRyxTx+ϵΦ. \\boxed{ \\Phi_\\triangle = \\frac{x^TRy}{x^Tx+\\epsilon_\\Phi}. } The global historical Φ\\Phi is the triangle average. 2. Joint Gaussian closure Assume the six-vector z=(x,y) z=(x,y) is jointly Gaussian with means mx,my m_x,\\qquad m_y and covariance blocks A=Cov(x,x),B=Cov(y,y),G=Cov(x,y). A=\\operatorname{Cov}(x,x), \\qquad B=\\operatorname{Cov}(y,y), \\qquad G=\\operatorname{Cov}(x,y). Only AA and GG are required for the conditional numerator. Gaussian conditioning gives E[y∣x]=my+GTA−1(x−mx). \\boxed{ E[y\\mid x] = m_y+ G^TA^{-1}(x-m_x). } Define L:=GTA−1,b:=my−Lmx, L:=G^TA^{-1}, \\qquad b:=m_y-Lm_x, and Qs:=12(RL+LTRT). Q_s := \\frac12 \\left( RL+L^TR^T \\right). Then E[xTRy∣x]=xTRb+xTQsx. E[x^TRy\\mid x] = x^TRb+x^TQ_sx. Therefore ΦG=E[xTRb+xTQsxxTx+ϵΦ]. \\Phi_G = E\\left[ \\frac{x^TRb+x^TQ_sx} {x^Tx+\\epsilon_\\Phi} \\right]. 3. Exact one-dimensional integral Use 1xTx+ϵΦ=∫0∞e−s(xTx+ϵΦ)ds. \\frac1{x^Tx+\\epsilon_\\Phi} = \\int_0^\\infty e^{-s(x^Tx+\\epsilon_\\Phi)} \\,ds. For x∼N(mx,A), x\\sim N(m_x,A), define Ms:=I+2sA, M_s:=I+2sA, ms:=Ms−1mx, m_s:=M_s^{-1}m_x, As:=AMs−1, A_s:=AM_s^{-1}, and Z(s)=det(Ms)−1/2exp[−smxTMs−1mx]. Z(s) = \\det(M_s)^{-1/2} \\exp\\left[ -s\\,m_x^TM_s^{-1}m_x \\right]. The exact Gaussian triadic-coherence functional is ΦG=∫0∞e−sϵΦZ(s)[msTRb+Tr(Qs[As+msmsT])]ds. \\boxed{ \\Phi_G = \\int_0^\\infty e^{-s\\epsilon_\\Phi} Z(s) \\left[ m_s^TRb + \\operatorname{Tr} \\left( Q_s[A_s+m_sm_s^T] \\right) \\right]ds. } This is a one-dimensional quadrature determined entirely by six-variable Gaussian first and second moments. No source Φ\\Phi value appears on the right-hand side. 4. Homogeneous triangular-lattice reduction For the source-homogeneous triangular lattice, mx=μt𝟏,my=μt−1𝟏. m_x=\\mu_t\\mathbf1, \\qquad m_y=\\mu_{t-1}\\mathbf1. All three vertices are mutually adjacent, so A=(C0C1C1C1C0C1C1C1C0). A= \\begin{pmatrix} C_0&C_1&C_1\\\\ C_1&C_0&C_1\\\\ C_1&C_1&C_0 \\end{pmatrix}. Similarly, defining same-site and nearest-neighbor one-step cross covariances G0=Ct,t−1(0),G1=Ct,t−1(e), G_0=C_{t,t-1}(0), \\qquad G_1=C_{t,t-1}(e), gives G=(G0G1G1G1G0G1G1G1G0). G= \\begin{pmatrix} G_0&G_1&G_1\\\\ G_1&G_0&G_1\\\\ G_1&G_1&G_0 \\end{pmatrix}. Thus the historical Φ\\Phi functional closes on only six scalar moments: ΦG=ℱΦ(μt,μt−1,C0,C1,G0,G1). \\boxed{ \\Phi_G = \\mathcal F_\\Phi( \\mu_t,\\mu_{t-1},C_0,C_1,G_0,G_1 ). } 5. Held-out candidate-model test of the functional The formula was frozen before candidate-model seeds 4001,…,4008 4001,\\ldots,4008 were evaluated. There are 8=23 8=2^3 held-out seeds. At ten declared source steps per seed, the Gaussian functional was supplied only the empirical first/second moments and compared with the exact historical ratio calculation. Across all held-out seeds: mean selected relative error=0.0017768 \\boxed{ \\text{mean selected relative error} = 0.0017768 } or approximately 0.178%. \\boxed{0.178\\%}. The maximum selected relative error over the full held-out set was 0.0103962 \\boxed{ 0.0103962 } or approximately 1.04%. \\boxed{1.04\\%}. Classification: HELDOUT_SIMULATION_PASS_WITHIN_V07_CANDIDATE_MODEL. This is not physical validation. 6. Important failure: functional closure is not full dynamical closure When the same Gaussian law is coupled to a fully autonomous second-moment/Hartree evolution, the early source trajectory is reproduced well but late-time dynamics drift substantially. For the seed-42 diagnostic: step 20: ΦGauto≈1.1139,Φsource≈1.1073; \\Phi_G^{auto}\\approx1.1139, \\qquad \\Phi^{source}\\approx1.1073; step 100: ΦGauto≈0.4166,Φsource≈0.5896; \\Phi_G^{auto}\\approx0.4166, \\qquad \\Phi^{source}\\approx0.5896; step 219: ΦGauto≈0.3383 \\boxed{ \\Phi_G^{auto}\\approx0.3383 } versus Φsource≈0.8825. \\boxed{ \\Phi^{source}\\approx0.8825. } The simultaneously autonomous Ω\\Omega and covariance also drift. However, if the actual first and second moments of the nonlinear replay are supplied to the Gaussian Φ\\Phi functional, the error remains below about 1%1\\% even at late times. Therefore the blocker is not primarily the ratio functional itself. It is the autonomous low-order moment dynamics. 7. New no-go / frontier AUTO_LOCK Φ is effectively closed as a six-moment Gaussian functional. \\boxed{ \\Phi \\text{ is effectively closed as a six-moment Gaussian functional.} } AUTO_LOCK NO-GO WITHIN CURRENT CLOSURE Gaussian-Hartree second-moment evolution alone⇏the full late nonlinear Genesis moment trajectory. \\boxed{ \\text{Gaussian-Hartree second-moment evolution alone} \\not\\Rightarrow \\text{the full late nonlinear Genesis moment trajectory.} } This is scoped to the current closure, not to all possible moment hierarchies. NEXT MATHEMATICAL BURDEN The surviving dynamics gap is higher-order moment transport generated by the quartic term and state-dependent feedback. Candidate next state variables include fourth-order cumulants or an explicitly non-Gaussian closure. PHYSICAL PROMOTION 0. \\boxed{0}. UD_MANUSCRIPT_ADDENDUM_PHI_TEMPORAL_v0.1.mdManuscript Addendum — Autonomous Triadic-Coherence Functional and Temporal Cluster Admission Artifact: UD_MANUSCRIPT_ADDENDUM_PHI_TEMPORAL_v0.1 Status: MILESTONE_PHI_FUNCTIONAL_CLOSURE / TEMPORAL-ADMISSION NO-GO Physical promotion: 0 The historical triadic-coherence observable has now been reduced to an autonomous Gaussian functional of the six-variable current/previous triangle law. For the current triangle vector xx and previous-step vector yy, the source quantity is Φ△=(xTRy)/(xTx+ϵΦ)\\Phi_\\triangle=(x^TRy)/(x^Tx+\\epsilon_\\Phi). Joint Gaussian conditioning reduces yy to an affine function of xx, after which a Laplace representation of the denominator produces an exact one-dimensional integral determined only by first and second moments. In the homogeneous triangular lattice this collapses to six scalar inputs: current and previous means, current same-site and nearest-neighbor covariances, and current/previous same-site and nearest-neighbor cross covariances. The functional itself performs strongly. On eight fresh candidate-model seeds, after the formula was frozen, the mean relative discrepancy from the exact historical triangle-ratio calculation is about 0.18%, with worst selected discrepancy about 1.04%. Yet a fully autonomous Gaussian-Hartree moment evolution drifts badly at late time. Supplying the actual nonlinear first and second moments restores the Φ\\Phi prediction. The surviving dynamics gap is therefore not the triadic ratio functional but the low-order autonomous moment hierarchy under quartic evolution. A temporal cluster-admission law was then derived from the source-driven covariance. The final-epoch same-site temporal covariance reaches e−1e^{-1} after 35 source steps, corresponding to 7/47/4 source time units, and crosses zero at 38 steps. Strict local peaks were tracked one-to-one between consecutive source states within the previously derived correlation radius r+=2r_+=2. A peak is admitted temporally only if its lifetime exceeds one e-fold coherence lag. This rule was frozen before eight new candidate-model seeds were executed. The e-fold lag remains exactly 35 steps across all eight seeds, demonstrating a stable temporal coherence scale, but the admitted final-object count is approximately 102 rather than 38. The combined result is another useful narrowing. Spatial covariance supplies a stable length scale and temporal covariance supplies a stable lifetime scale, but neither alone defines the historical cluster ontology. A future cluster law must operate on a joint spatiotemporal object—most plausibly including basin mass/energy, lifetime, and closure/admissibility—rather than on raw peaks alone. Physical promotion remains zero. UD_TEMPORAL_COHERENCE_CLUSTER_ADMISSION_v0.1.mdUD_TEMPORAL_COHERENCE_CLUSTER_ADMISSION_v0.1 Date: 2026-09-13 Purpose: derive a temporal cluster-admission law from peak lifetime/coherence rather than spatial scale alone Status: TEMPORAL_COHERENCE_SCALE_DERIVED__ONE_TO_ONE_TRACK_LAW_TESTED__SIMPLE_LIFETIME_ADMISSION_NO_GO_FOR_38 Physical promotion: 0 1. Motivation The covariance lane already fixes a robust spatial correlation radius r+=2. r_+=2. Spatial scale alone does not reconstruct the historical 38-cluster receipt. The next admissibility question is therefore temporal: Does a localized amplitude maximum persist long enough to count as a coherent object rather than a transient fluctuation? 2. Source-derived temporal coherence Let Ctt′(0)=E[ηi(t)ηi(t′)] C_{tt'}(0) = E[\\eta_i(t)\\eta_i(t')] be the same-site cross-time covariance generated by the non-autonomous Genesis covariance propagator. Define normalized final-epoch temporal coherence ρT(ℓ)=Ctf,tf−ℓ(0)Ctf,tf(0)Ctf−ℓ,tf−ℓ(0). \\boxed{ \\rho_T(\\ell) = \\frac{ C_{t_f,t_f-\\ell}(0) }{ \\sqrt{ C_{t_f,t_f}(0) C_{t_f-\\ell,t_f-\\ell}(0) } }. } Define the e-fold coherence lag ℓe=min{ℓ≥0:ρT(ℓ)≤e−1}. \\boxed{ \\ell_e = \\min\\{ \\ell\\ge0: \\rho_T(\\ell)\\le e^{-1} \\}. } Define the first-zero lag ℓ0=min{ℓ≥0:ρT(ℓ)≤0}. \\boxed{ \\ell_0 = \\min\\{ \\ell\\ge0: \\rho_T(\\ell)\\le0 \\}. } For seed 42 at the candidate final epoch: ℓe=35=5⋅7 \\boxed{ \\ell_e=35=5\\cdot7 } source steps and ℓ0=38=2⋅19 \\boxed{ \\ell_0=38=2\\cdot19 } steps. With source time step h=0.05=120, h=0.05=\\frac1{20}, the e-fold time is τe=35h=74=1.75 \\boxed{ \\tau_e = 35h = \\frac74 = 1.75 } source time units. The first-zero time is τ0=38h=1910=1.9. \\boxed{ \\tau_0 = 38h = \\frac{19}{10} = 1.9. } These values are derived from covariance, not from the historical cluster count. 3. One-to-one peak tracks At each source step, define candidate objects as strict local maxima of |ai|. |a_i|. Between consecutive steps, permit a continuation only within the already-derived spatial covariance radius r+=2. r_+=2. A deterministic one-to-one matching is applied in this priority order: minimum graph distance; minimum normalized change in absolute peak amplitude; node index as a final deterministic tie-break. Unmatched new peaks are births. Unmatched old peaks terminate. This prevents multiple final peaks from sharing one historical ancestor. 4. Temporal admission law Define lifetime in source states as Lα=tdeath−tbirth+1. L_\\alpha = t_{\\mathrm death}-t_{\\mathrm birth}+1. The proposed e-fold admission rule is: ADMITT(α)⇔Lα≥ℓe+1. \\boxed{ \\text{ADMIT}_T(\\alpha) \\iff L_\\alpha\\ge\\ell_e+1. } The +1+1 converts lag count to number of sampled states. A stricter zero-crossing control is Lα≥ℓ0+1. \\boxed{ L_\\alpha\\ge\\ell_0+1. } No free lifetime multiplier is introduced. 5. Seed-42 result Seed 42 has 352 352 final strict maxima. Under one-to-one tracking, final-track lifetime has: median: 32 states; 32\\text{ states}; mean: 40.75 states; 40.75\\text{ states}; maximum: 221 states. 221\\text{ states}. E-fold admission gives 106 \\boxed{ 106 } final temporally coherent peaks. First-zero admission gives 103. \\boxed{ 103. } Thus temporal coherence is a meaningful discriminator, but it does not reproduce 38. 6. Fresh held-out candidate-model test The temporal rule was frozen before candidate-model seeds 5001,…,5008 5001,\\ldots,5008 were executed. Again, 8=23. 8=2^3. For all eight held-out seeds: ℓe=35 \\boxed{ \\ell_e=35 } exactly. Thus the covariance-defined temporal coherence scale is highly stable in this candidate model. Held-out final strict peaks: 359.0±10.17 \\boxed{ 359.0\\pm10.17 } with range 336336 to 368368. E-fold admitted tracks: 102.25±9.58 \\boxed{ 102.25\\pm9.58 } with range 91 to 120. 91\\text{ to }120. First-zero admitted tracks: 101.0±8.99 \\boxed{ 101.0\\pm8.99 } with range 91 to 119. 91\\text{ to }119. No held-out seed returns 38 under either rule. 7. Result Temporal coherence closes another ambiguity: many spatial peaks are shorter-lived than one source coherence time. \\boxed{ \\text{many spatial peaks are shorter-lived than one source coherence time.} } But: one coherence-time lifetime threshold is insufficient to reconstruct the historical 38-cluster ontology. \\boxed{ \\text{one coherence-time lifetime threshold is insufficient to reconstruct the historical 38-cluster ontology.} } Combined with the previous spatial result: spatial covariance-overlap gives roughly 2828 objects; temporal e-fold lifetime gives roughly 102102 objects. Therefore historical cluster identity is not determined by either spatial correlation scale or peak lifetime alone. A joint object-level law is required. Likely load-bearing quantities are: basin lifetime and basin mass/energy; temporal persistence of an already spatially merged component; closure/admissibility of a basin; saddle-flux persistence through time. 8. Governance AUTO_LOCK ℓe=35 \\ell_e=35 as the stable candidate-model temporal coherence lag under the declared covariance definition. HELDOUT SIMULATION RESULT The one-to-one e-fold temporal rule robustly yields approximately 10210^2 final coherent peaks, not 38. NO-GO simple peak lifetime/coherence alone does not recover the historical 38 clusters. \\boxed{ \\text{simple peak lifetime/coherence alone does not recover the historical 38 clusters.} } AUTO_FREEZE fitting a lifetime multiplier to obtain 38; changing the track radius after seeing the result; combining temporal and spatial rules post hoc without preregistration. OPEN A preregistered joint spatiotemporal object law. PHYSICAL PROMOTION 0. \\boxed{0}."
