ed by an independent channel. The protocol keeps winding labels, phase-slip admission, calorimetry, and model scoring strictly separate and hash-freezes the raw data before any comparison. I can provide a concise laboratory handoff packet with calibration schemas, event records, timing requirements, and blinded schedules. Would you be open to a short technical discussion about the feasibility of such a hybrid ring-plus-calorimeter device or collaboration? Best regards, Benjamin Mayes C. Jukka Pekola — Aalto PICO Recipient: jukka.pekola@aalto.fi Subject: Technical feasibility: millikelvin thermometry for a blinded 2→32\\to3 superconducting phase-slip experiment Professor Pekola, I’m developing a blinded, target-independent superconducting phase-slip experiment that would qualify consecutive winding states n=2n=2 and n=3n=3 in an isolated ring before measuring a single 2→32\\to3 transition. PICO’s work in electronic thermometry, superconducting nanostructures, and phase-slip calorimetry makes your group an especially relevant partner for the thermal and uncertainty side of the experiment. The protocol requires state labels to come from an independent readout, with calorimetry treated only as a separate event receipt—not as a way to label or select the transition. I have frozen schemas for calibration, synchronization, state qualification, raw-data custody, and a balanced blinded event schedule. I’d be grateful for a technical assessment of whether PICO’s current thermometry/bolometry methods could be integrated with a state-resolved superconducting-ring platform, either at Aalto or through a collaboration. The immediate goal is experimental feasibility and falsifiability, not theory endorsement. Best regards, Benjamin Mayes D. Ioan Pop — KIT Recipient: ioan.pop@kit.edu Subject: Feasibility question: adapting superconducting-state QND readout to multi-winding ring qualification Professor Pop, Your 2026 work on coherent manipulation and QND readout of superconducting vo